  read, nothing to clean up, no way for the two to disagree.

  The alternative — storing notification objects when a deadline passes — buys you dismissal and history, and costs you
  a second source of truth that has to stay in sync with tasks, plus dedupe logic so one task doesn't generate a
  reminder every tick. I don't think it's worth it here, and it's easy to add later if you miss it. So: [1mno dismiss [0m
  [1mbutton.[0m Deliberate, not an omission.

  [1mDue dates are dates, not timestamps.[0m [38;5;153mdueDate[39m is a [38;5;153mYYYY-MM-DD[39m string. People think about task deadlines in days, and
  date-only sidesteps a class of timezone and clock-skew bugs entirely. Buckets, computed against the user's local
  calendar day:

  - [1mOverdue[0m — [38;5;153mdueDate[39m is before today
  - [1mDue today[0m — [38;5;153mdueDate[39m equals today
  - [1mDue soon[0m — within the next 3 days
  - [1mLater[0m — everything else, plus tasks with no due date

  Completed tasks are excluded from every bucket regardless of date.

  [1mWhat you see:[0m a summary line at the top of [38;5;153m<main>[39m — "2 overdue · 1 due today" — with overdue styled to actually draw
  the eye, and nothing at all shown when nothing is urgent. Silence when there's no news is a feature. Below it, the
  task list with per-task due-date styling so the list itself carries the signal.

  The one-minute tick means a task due today becomes overdue at midnight while the page sits open, without a refresh.
